<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spiro Mounds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–<a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Spiro Mounds is an archaeological site on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> River in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, a centre of the Caddoan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture</a> from about 800 to 1450. Craig Mound, one of its twelve earthen mounds, held a hollow burial chamber, the Great Mortuary, that preserved textiles, feathers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and engraved conch shell cups and gorgets. Most people moved to nearby villages around 1250, but Spiro remained a ceremonial centre and burial ground. Treasure hunters who tunnelled into Craig Mound in the 1930s sold its contents to collectors, prompting a state law protecting the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spiro-mounds.jpg" alt="Spiro Mounds" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herb Roe, www.chromesun.com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spiro_Big_Boy_effigy_pipe_HRoe_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
